  "seoDescription": "Append one key and value position to a KV cache and compute grouped-query attention for the next token.",
  "description": "Append new single-token key and value vectors to the persistent KV cache along the sequence axis and compute grouped-query scaled dot-product attention for autoregressive decoding.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}_{\\text{new}} = [\\mathbf{K}_{\\text{past}}; \\mathbf{k}_t], \\quad \\mathbf{V}_{\\text{new}} = [\\mathbf{V}_{\\text{past}}; \\mathbf{v}_t], \\quad \\mathbf{y}_t = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{q}_t \\mathbf{K}_{\\text{new}}^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\mathbf{V}_{\\text{new}}\n$$\n\n**Step-by-Step Implementation:**\n1. Concatenate new key and value vectors to the historical key-value cache along the sequence length dimension.\n2. Compute query-key affinity dot products scaled by 1 / sqrt(d_k).\n3. Apply numerically stable softmax normalization along the keys dimension.\n4. Multiply attention weights by the updated value cache.\n5. Return attention output along with updated k_cache and v_cache.\n\n**Returns:** Dictionary with 'output', 'k_cache', and 'v_cache'.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: x = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\nOutput: result matching Decode Attention with a KV Cache specification."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Must implement `cs336_l10_kv_cache_decode_step` using PYTORCH.\n* Must preserve input dimensions and return correct output types.\n* Vectorized and numerically stable.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Decode Attention with a KV Cache\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nAppend new single-token key and value vectors to the persistent KV cache along the sequence axis and compute grouped-query scaled dot-product attention for autoregressive decoding. In modern deep learning and large-scale AI architectures, numerical correctness, memory efficiency, and deterministic computation form the foundational substrate for stable model training and fast inference. Understanding how each mathematical operator maps to low-level tensor algorithms enables practitioners to diagnose bottlenecks, eliminate silent numerical bugs, and design scalable custom operators.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe mathematical formulation governing this operation is given by:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}_{\\text{new}} = [\\mathbf{K}_{\\text{past}}; \\mathbf{k}_t], \\quad \\mathbf{V}_{\\text{new}} = [\\mathbf{V}_{\\text{past}}; \\mathbf{v}_t], \\quad \\mathbf{y}_t = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{q}_t \\mathbf{K}_{\\text{new}}^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\mathbf{V}_{\\text{new}}\n$$\n\nEach variable and term plays a precise functional role. In neural network training, preserving dimensional consistency across batch, sequence, and feature channels is essential. Numerical precision and dynamic range must be carefully preserved to avoid catastrophic floating-point cancellation, gradient underflow, or numerical instability.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Dimension Setup**: Inspect tensor shapes, ensure contiguous memory layouts, and validate bounds.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Mathematical Transformation**: Concatenate new key and value vectors to the historical key-value cache along the sequence length dimension.\n3. **Phase 3: Iterative Execution & Reduction**: Compute query-key affinity dot products scaled by 1 / sqrt(d_k).\n4. **Phase 4: Output Assembly & Precision Guarantee**: Return attention output along with updated k_cache and v_cache.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Scalability & Performance**: Vectorized execution minimizes Python interpreter overhead and saturates modern SIMD and GPU hardware execution units.\n* **Numerical Stability**: Defensive handling of small denominators, extreme exponentials, and boundary tails prevents NaN propagation during large distributed training runs.\n* **Production Reliability**: Deterministic implementations guarantee reproducibility across different evaluation batches and distributed parallel ranks.",
